---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const defaultFunction = `f <- function(x) {
  exp(-x^2)
}`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Simpson's Rule — CMNA"
  description="Experiment with Simpson's rule using arbitrary R functions in the CMNA Laboratory."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Numerical integration · CMNA Laboratory</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Simpson's rule</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Replace the integrand with local quadratics, integrate those easy curves,
        and add the pieces. Then do it to your own R function.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-column">
      <article class="content">
        <h2>Three samples define the panel</h2>
        <p>
          CMNA's <code>simp()</code> divides the interval into <code>m</code> panels.
          For each panel it evaluates the function at the left endpoint, midpoint,
          and right endpoint. Those three values determine a quadratic approximation,
          whose area is
        </p>
        <p class="display-equation"><code>h / 6 × [f(x₀) + 4f(xₘ) + f(x₁)]</code></p>
        <p>
          The factor of four on the midpoint is not decoration: it is exactly the
          weight produced by integrating the interpolating quadratic.
        </p>
      </article>
      <aside class="callout">
        <strong>What to watch</strong>
        <p>
          The dark blue line is the function R actually evaluates. Gold panels show
          the local quadratic approximations. Play through them one at a time and
          watch the accumulated area approach the final Simpson estimate.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section lab-section">
    <div class="shell" data-simpson-lab>
      <div class="lab-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="section-kicker">Bring your own integrand</p>
          <h2 class="section-title">Let R draw the curve. Let Simpson tile it.</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            Write any vectorized R function <code>f(x)</code>, choose bounds and a
            panel count, then play the approximation panel by panel.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="lab-expression">
          <span>Runtime</span>
          <code data-status>Preparing R…</code>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="r-guided-grid">
        <section class="r-guided-editor">
          <div class="r-editor-label"><span>R</span><small>Define <code>f(x)</code></small></div>
          <textarea class="r-editor r-function-editor" data-r-function spellcheck="false">{defaultFunction}</textarea>
          <div class="r-guided-controls">
            <label>a<input type="number" value="0" step="any" data-a /></label>
            <label>b<input type="number" value="2" step="any" data-b /></label>
            <label>panels m<input type="number" value="8" min="1" max="100" step="1" data-m /></label>
          </div>
          <div class="r-run-row">
            <button class="button r-run" type="button" data-run disabled>Run Simpson in R</button>
            <button class="r-reset" type="button" data-reset>Reset R session</button>
          </div>
        </section>
        <aside class="r-guided-primer">
          <h3>What changes when m grows?</h3>
          <p>
            More panels make each quadratic local to a smaller piece of the function.
            For a smooth integrand this usually improves the approximation, while
            also requiring more function evaluations.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </div>

      <div class="lab-summary" data-summary></div>

      <div class="lab-plot-frame">
        <svg class="lab-plot" role="img" aria-label="Function with Simpson quadratic panels" data-plot></svg>
      </div>

      <div class="lab-stepper">
        <button class="button secondary-on-light" type="button" data-play disabled>Play panels</button>
        <label><span data-step-label>Panel</span><input type="range" min="0" max="1" value="0" data-step disabled /></label>
      </div>
      <p class="lab-status" data-step-detail></p>

      <div class="lab-table-wrap">
        <table class="lab-table">
          <thead><tr><th>panel</th><th>x₀</th><th>xₘ</th><th>x₁</th><th>panel area</th><th>running area</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody data-rows></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script is:inline type="module" src="/js/simpson-lab.js"></script>
</BaseLayout>
